  <title>Practice problems</title>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-sec-e-6-practice-problems-graphing-windows">
    <title>Graphing windows</title>

    <p><ol>
      <li>
        <p>The graph of</p>
          <p><me>f(x)=x^3-6x</me></p>
        <p>is shown only in the window</p>
          <p><me>-1\le x\le1,\qquad -6\le y\le6.</me></p>
        <p>Explain why this window does not show the full shape of the graph.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>A calculator graphs</p>
          <p><me>g(x)=\frac{x^2-4}{x-2}</me></p>
        <p>as a straight line.  What feature might the graph be hiding?</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>Find a viewing window that shows both local extrema of</p>
          <p><me>f(x)=x^3-3x.</me></p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>Explain why a graph of</p>
          <p><me>y=\sin(100x)</me></p>
        <p>may be misleading if too few sample points are used.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>A graphing tool connects points across a vertical asymptote.  What algebraic or
calculus information should you check?</p>
      </li>
    </ol></p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-sec-e-6-practice-problems-numerical-precision-and-roundoff">
    <title>Numerical precision and roundoff</title>

    <p><ol>
      <li>
        <p>Explain why</p>
          <p><me>\frac{\sqrt{1+x}-1}{x}</me></p>
        <p>can be numerically unstable for <m>x</m> near <m>0</m>.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>Rewrite</p>
          <p><me>\frac{\sqrt{1+x}-1}{x}</me></p>
        <p>in a more stable form for <m>x\neq0</m>.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>In exact arithmetic,</p>
          <p><me>(10^{16}+1)-10^{16}=1.</me></p>
        <p>Why might a finite-precision machine fail to return <m>1</m>?</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>In a difference quotient</p>
          <p><me>\frac{f(a+h)-f(a)}{h},</me></p>
        <p>what happens if <m>h</m> is too large?  What can happen if <m>h</m> is too small?</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>Describe the difference between truncation error and roundoff error.</p>
      </li>
    </ol></p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-sec-e-6-practice-problems-calculator-and-cas-syntax">
    <title>Calculator and CAS syntax</title>

    <p><ol>
      <li>
        <p>What expression is represented by</p>
          <p><me>\texttt{1/x+1}?</me></p>
        <p>How should you type</p>
          <p><me>\frac1{x+1}?</me></p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>In Python, what is wrong with typing</p>
        <p><c>x^2</c></p>
        <p>if you intend <m>x^2</m>?</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>Explain the difference between</p>
          <p><me>\texttt{exp(-x**2)}</me></p>
        <p>and</p>
          <p><me>\texttt{exp(-x)**2}.</me></p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>Why should a calculator be in radian mode when checking</p>
          <p><me>\frac{d}{dx}\sin x=\cos x?</me></p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>A CAS returns</p>
          <p><me>\log(x)</me></p>
        <p>for</p>
          <p><me>\int \frac1x\,dx.</me></p>
        <p>What real-domain issue should you remember?</p>
      </li>
    </ol></p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-sec-e-6-practice-problems-simple-python-experiments">
    <title>Simple Python experiments</title>

    <p><ol>
      <li>
        <p>Write Python code to compute</p>
          <p><me>\frac{(2+h)^2-2^2}{h}</me></p>
        <p>for</p>
          <p><me>h=1,\ 0.1,\ 0.01,\ 0.001.</me></p>
        <p>What value do the results approach?</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>Modify the bisection code to find a root of</p>
          <p><me>f(x)=x^2-2</me></p>
        <p>on <m>[1,2]</m>.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>Use the midpoint rule to approximate</p>
          <p><me>\int_0^1 x^2\,dx</me></p>
        <p>with <m>n=10</m>.  Compare with the exact value.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>Use Euler’s method with step size <m>h=0.1</m> to approximate the solution of</p>
          <p><me>y'=y,\qquad y(0)=1</me></p>
        <p>at <m>t=1</m>.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>Use Newton’s method to approximate <m>\sqrt2</m> by applying the method to</p>
          <p><me>f(x)=x^2-2.</me></p>
      </li>
    </ol></p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-sec-e-6-practice-problems-plotting-sequences-sums-and-taylor-polynomials">
    <title>Plotting sequences, sums, and Taylor polynomials</title>

    <p><ol>
      <li>
        <p>Plot the first <m>30</m> terms of</p>
          <p><me>a_n=\frac{n}{n+1}.</me></p>
        <p>What limit does the plot suggest?</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>Compute and plot the partial sums of</p>
          <p><me>\sum_{k=1}^{\infty}\frac1{k^2}</me></p>
        <p>for <m>1\le n\le100</m>.  Do the partial sums appear to settle?</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>Compute and plot the partial sums of the harmonic series</p>
          <p><me>\sum_{k=1}^{\infty}\frac1k</me></p>
        <p>for <m>1\le n\le1000</m>.  Why can the plot be misleading?</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>Plot</p>
          <p><me>\sin x,
\qquad
T_1(x)=x,
\qquad
T_3(x)=x-\frac{x^3}{3!},
\qquad
T_5(x)=x-\frac{x^3}{3!}+\frac{x^5}{5!}</me></p>
        <p>on <m>[-4,4]</m>.  Where do the Taylor polynomials give the best approximation?</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>For</p>
          <p><me>e^x=\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\frac{x^n}{n!},</me></p>
        <p>write code to compute the Maclaurin polynomial</p>
          <p><me>T_N(x)=\sum_{n=0}^{N}\frac{x^n}{n!}.</me></p>
        <p>Compare <m>T_N(1)</m> with <m>e</m> for <m>N=1,2,3,4,5,10</m>.</p>
      </li>
    </ol></p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="ans-appendix-e-practice">
    <title>Answers to Appendix E practice problems</title>


  <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-sec-e-6-practice-problems-graphing-windows-2">
    <title>Graphing windows</title>

    <p><ol>
      <li>
        <p>The window <m>[-1,1]</m> in <m>x</m> does not include the critical points of</p>
          <p><me>f(x)=x^3-6x.</me></p>
        <p>Since</p>
          <p><me>f'(x)=3x^2-6=3(x^2-2),</me></p>
        <p>the critical points are</p>
          <p><me>x=\pm\sqrt2\approx \pm1.414.</me></p>
        <p>They lie outside the small window.  A wider window such as <m>[-3,3]</m> shows the
rise-fall-rise behavior.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
          <p><me>g(x)=\frac{x^2-4}{x-2}
=
\frac{(x-2)(x+2)}{x-2}
=
x+2,
\qquad x\neq2.</me></p>
        <p>The graph is the line <m>y=x+2</m> with a hole at</p>
          <p><me>(2,4).</me></p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>For</p>
          <p><me>f(x)=x^3-3x,</me></p>
        <p>we have</p>
          <p><me>f'(x)=3x^2-3=3(x-1)(x+1).</me></p>
        <p>So the critical points are</p>
          <p><me>x=-1,\qquad x=1.</me></p>
        <p>A window such as</p>
          <p><me>-2.5\le x\le2.5,
\qquad
-4.5\le y\le4.5</me></p>
        <p>shows both local extrema.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>The function <m>\sin(100x)</m> oscillates rapidly.  If the graphing tool samples too few
points, it may miss peaks and troughs between sample points and draw a misleading
connected curve.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>Check where the denominator is zero, compute one-sided limits, and identify vertical
asymptotes.  The graph should not be trusted across inputs where the function is
undefined.</p>
      </li>
    </ol></p>
  </paragraphs>

  <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-sec-e-6-practice-problems-numerical-precision-and-roundoff-2">
    <title>Numerical precision and roundoff</title>

    <p><ol>
      <li>
        <p>For <m>x</m> near <m>0</m>, the two numbers</p>
          <p><me>\sqrt{1+x}
\quad\text{and}\quad
1</me></p>
        <p>are very close.  Subtracting nearly equal stored numbers can lose significant digits.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>Rationalize:</p>
          <p><me>\frac{\sqrt{1+x}-1}{x}
\cdot
\frac{\sqrt{1+x}+1}{\sqrt{1+x}+1}
=
\frac{1}{\sqrt{1+x}+1},
\qquad x\neq0.</me></p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>A finite-precision machine may not store <m>10^{16}+1</m> distinctly from <m>10^{16}</m>.
If both are stored as the same floating-point number, their computed difference is <m>0</m>,
not <m>1</m>.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>If <m>h</m> is too large, the secant slope may be a poor approximation to the tangent
slope.  This is truncation error.  If <m>h</m> is too small, the subtraction</p>
          <p><me>f(a+h)-f(a)</me></p>
        <p>may lose significant digits.  This is roundoff error.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>Truncation error comes from replacing an infinite or limiting process by a finite one.
Roundoff error comes from storing and computing with finitely many digits.</p>
      </li>
    </ol></p>
  </paragraphs>

  <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-sec-e-6-practice-problems-calculator-and-cas-syntax-2">
    <title>Calculator and CAS syntax</title>

    <p><ol>
      <li>
          <p><me>\texttt{1/x+1}</me></p>
        <p>means</p>
          <p><me>\frac1x+1.</me></p>
        <p>To type</p>
          <p><me>\frac1{x+1},</me></p>
        <p>use</p>
          <p><me>\texttt{1/(x+1)}.</me></p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>In ordinary Python numerical code, <c>^</c> is not exponentiation.  Use</p>
          <p><me>\texttt{x**2}.</me></p>
      </li>
      <li>
          <p><me>\texttt{exp(-x**2)}</me></p>
        <p>means</p>
          <p><me>e^{-x^2}.</me></p>
        <p>But</p>
          <p><me>\texttt{exp(-x)**2}</me></p>
        <p>means</p>
          <p><me>(e^{-x})^2=e^{-2x}.</me></p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>The derivative formula</p>
          <p><me>(\sin x)'=\cos x</me></p>
        <p>is true when <m>x</m> is measured in radians.  Degree mode changes the scale of the input
and therefore changes the numerical derivative.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>For real-valued calculus,</p>
          <p><me>\int \frac1x\,dx=\ln|x|+C.</me></p>
        <p>A CAS output of <m>\log(x)</m> usually assumes <m>x&gt;0</m>, or it is working in a context
where branch choices are being handled differently.</p>
      </li>
    </ol></p>
  </paragraphs>

  <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-sec-e-6-practice-problems-simple-python-experiments-2">
    <title>Simple Python experiments</title>

    <p><ol>
      <li>
        <p>The quotient is</p>
          <p><me>\frac{(2+h)^2-2^2}{h}=4+h.</me></p>
        <p>For</p>
          <p><me>h=1,\ 0.1,\ 0.01,\ 0.001,</me></p>
        <p>the values are</p>
          <p><me>5,\quad 4.1,\quad 4.01,\quad 4.001.</me></p>
        <p>They approach <m>4</m>.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>Use the same bisection structure with</p>
          <pre>
def f(x):
    return x**2 - 2

left = 1.0
right = 2.0
  </pre>
        <p>The midpoint values approach</p>
          <p><me>\sqrt2\approx1.41421356.</me></p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>For <m>f(x)=x^2</m> on <m>[0,1]</m>, the exact value is</p>
          <p><me>\int_0^1 x^2\,dx=\frac13.</me></p>
        <p>The midpoint rule with <m>n=10</m> uses</p>
          <p><me>\Delta x=0.1</me></p>
        <p>and midpoints</p>
          <p><me>0.05,\ 0.15,\ldots,\ 0.95.</me></p>
        <p>The approximation is</p>
          <p><me>M_{10}=0.3325.</me></p>
        <p>It is close to</p>
          <p><me>\frac13\approx0.333333.</me></p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>Euler’s method for</p>
          <p><me>y'=y,\qquad y(0)=1</me></p>
        <p>with <m>h=0.1</m> uses</p>
          <p><me>y_{n+1}=y_n+0.1y_n=1.1y_n.</me></p>
        <p>After <m>10</m> steps,</p>
          <p><me>y_{10}=1.1^{10}\approx2.593742.</me></p>
        <p>The exact value is</p>
          <p><me>e^1=e\approx2.718282.</me></p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>For</p>
          <p><me>f(x)=x^2-2,
\qquad
f'(x)=2x,</me></p>
        <p>Newton’s method is</p>
          <p><me>x_{n+1}
=
x_n-\frac{x_n^2-2}{2x_n}
=
\frac12\left(x_n+\frac2{x_n}\right).</me></p>
        <p>Starting with <m>x_0=1.5</m>, the iterates quickly approach</p>
          <p><me>\sqrt2\approx1.41421356.</me></p>
      </li>
    </ol></p>
  </paragraphs>

  <paragraphs xml:id="subsec-sec-e-6-practice-problems-plotting-sequences-sums-and-taylor-polynomials-2">
    <title>Plotting sequences, sums, and Taylor polynomials</title>

    <p><ol>
      <li>
          <p><me>a_n=\frac{n}{n+1}=1-\frac1{n+1}.</me></p>
        <p>The plot should suggest</p>
          <p><me>a_n\to1.</me></p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>The partial sums of</p>
          <p><me>\sum_{k=1}^{\infty}\frac1{k^2}</me></p>
        <p>increase and appear to settle.  The series converges.  Its exact sum is</p>
          <p><me>\frac{\pi^2}{6},</me></p>
        <p>though that exact result is beyond the usual first test for convergence.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>The harmonic partial sums grow very slowly.  A plot up to <m>n=1000</m> may look as if
the sums are settling, but the harmonic series diverges.  This is a classic example where
numerical evidence can mislead.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>The Taylor polynomials give the best approximation near the center</p>
          <p><me>x=0.</me></p>
        <p>Farther from <m>0</m>, low-degree Taylor polynomials become less reliable.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>The code should compute</p>
          <p><me>T_N(1)=\sum_{n=0}^{N}\frac1{n!}.</me></p>
        <p>As <m>N</m> increases, these values approach</p>
          <p><me>e\approx2.718281828.</me></p>
        <p>For example,</p>
          <p><me>T_1(1)=2,</me></p>
          <p><me>T_2(1)=2.5,</me></p>
          <p><me>T_3(1)=2.666\ldots,</me></p>
        <p>and</p>
          <p><me>T_{10}(1)</me></p>
        <p>is already very close to <m>e</m>.</p>
      </li>
    </ol></p>
  </paragraphs>

</subsection>

